A1. This is the spirit of the thing. ~ ALCOHOL
A6. This is a gin. ~ HOLLAND
A12. A female fish of the salmon. ~ RAUN
A14. The prices of drunkenness. ~ FEES
A15. How you feel when you get the bill. ~ IRATE
A17. Decorated lettering at the beginning of a chapter—in vogue in 17th and 18th Centuries. ~ FAC
A19. This old boy told a lot of fairy stories. ~ AESOP
A21. Came together—as for a drink. ~ MET
A22. Put this in Scotch and you have a highball. ~ SELTZER
A25. A speak-easy detector. ~ EAR
A26. Where Volstead put his act. ~ OVER
A28. Island built by animalcule. ~ CORAL
A29. This means to be against—(not necessarily a bar). ~ ANTI
A30. If you do this too long you'll be full. ~ SUP
A32. A boat propeller. ~ OAR
A33. This means to be on one's dignity; in a haughty mood. ~ ALT
A34. A period of time (abbr.). ~ YR
A36. A tract of waste land. ~ MOOR
A38. What the scofflaw says when asked to have a drink. ~ SURE
A40. To have existence. ~ BE
A41. A definite article in Old England. ~ YE
A42. A little conjunction that causes lots of trouble. ~ IF
A43. What JUDGE thinks of prohibition (init.). ~ NG
A44. A continent (init.). ~ SA
A45. Small livestock of South Africa. ~ FE
A46. These are busy little things (not Federal agents). ~ ANTS
A48. A disturbance often caused by "bootleg." ~ RIOT
A50. The district in which prohibition was passed (init.). ~ DC
A52. What most any old hen is apt to do. ~ SET
A53. The length of time that, it seems, has passed since the good old Pre-Volstead days. ~ ERA
A55. A lofty, jutting rock. ~ TOR
A57. Eve's lingerie. ~ LEAF
A59. Organizations that are usually very wet. ~ FRATS
A61. It's hard to find this kind of liquor since prohibition. ~ PURE
A63. This liquid makes a most unpleasant drink. ~ OIL
A64. A fellow who used to do a lot of setting-up exercises. ~ BARKEEP
A66. An American humorist. (Not Wayne B. Wheeler). ~ NYE
A67. A Greek woman who loved her children. ~ NIOBE
A69. What to say when offered a cocktail. ~ YES
A70. This means "on the inside." ~ INNER
A72. Cereal grass. ~ OATS
A74. A good old beer drinking organization (init.). ~ BPOE
A75. What prohibition does to the average citizen. ~ ENRAGE
A76. A part of the old Manhattan cocktail. ~ CHERRY

D1. The price of freedom. ~ ALIMONY
D2. Protective frameworks used in shipping things. ~ CRATES
D3. A grain. ~ OAT
D4. Great shoutings or clamors—such as in a raid. ~ HUES
D5. A preposition. ~ ON
D7. A preposition. ~ OF
D8. An old king Shakespeare wrote about. ~ LEAR
D9. A Southern General. ~ LEE
D10. An easy thing to do when asked "to have one on the house." ~ ASSENT
D11. What prohibition fails to do. ~ DEPRIVE
D13. A mountain range in Hungary ~ MATRA
D16. A title given to clergymen (abbr.). ~ REV
D17. A resting place for inebriates. ~ FLOOR
D18. These people liked Vodka. ~ CZARS
D20. An edible grain. ~ OAT
D23. Elevating cider—(init.). ~ EC
D24. A definite article in Spain. ~ EL
D27. A liquor distilled from molasses or cane juice. ~ RUM
D29. A favorite drink in England. ~ ALE
D31. This is sharp. ~ POINT
D33. A jargon; slang. ~ ARGOT
D35. Use this in an "Old Fashioned Cocktail." ~ RYE
D37. Frequently. ~ OFT
D39. One (Latin)—a combining form. ~ UNI
D40. Lots of this kind of liquor since prohibition. ~ BAD
D45. Criminals (not beer drinkers). ~ FELONS
D46. An organization which fought for freedom—and got prohibition (abbr.). ~ AEF
D47. To press together, as in ranks. ~ SERRY
D48. Standards by which values are adjusted. ~ RATES
D49. This goes around a lot. ~ TOP
D51. What we'll hear when prohibition is repealed. ~ CHEERS
D52. Where the schooners slide over the bar. ~ SALOON
D54. Night club habitués. ~ RAKES
D56. If you're a good one you can make your get-away. ~ RUNNER
D58. East Indian Inhibitions (init.). ~ EII
D59. A note in the musical scale. ~ FA
D60. A point on the compass. ~ SE
D62. Good for a highball. ~ RYE
D64. A Greek letter. ~ BETA
D65. A great aid to a pipe-dream. ~ PIPE
D68. Where the "line of least resistance" lines up. ~ BAR
D71. A very negative conjunction. ~ NOR
D73. Specific gravity (init.)—you're welcome! ~ SG
D74. A summer resort in Maine—(init.). ~ BH


